```uvl
{
  "title": "PC Configuration Guide",
  "description": "A tool to guide users through building a PC, considering component compatibility.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "CPU Selection",
      "content": "The CPU choice (e.g., Intel vs. AMD) determines the required motherboard socket (e.g., LGA 1700 vs. AM5)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Motherboard & RAM",
      "content": "The motherboard chipset (e.g., Z790) dictates the supported RAM type (DDR4 or DDR5)."
    },
    {
      "title": "GPU Power & Size",
      "content": "A high-end GPU (e.g., RTX 4090) requires a minimum power supply (PSU) of 850W or higher. The GPU's physical length (e.g., 350mm) must be compatible with the case's maximum GPU clearance."
    },
    {
      "title": "Case & Cooler Compatibility",
      "content": "The case (e.g., ATX, Micro-ATX) must match the motherboard's form factor.  If using a large air cooler, its height must be less than the case's CPU cooler clearance."
    }
  ]
}
```

**Explanation of the UVL code:**

*   **`{}`**:  The outermost curly braces define the root object of the UVL document.
*   **`"title"`**:  A string representing the title of the guide.
*   **`"description"`**: A brief description of the guide.
*   **`"sections"`**: An array of sections, each representing a part of the guide.
*   **`"title"`**:  A string representing the title of each section.
*   **`"content"`**:  A string containing the textual explanation of each section.
*   **`"e.g."`**: I've kept the examples (e.g., Intel vs. AMD) within the text for clarity.  UVL doesn't have a direct equivalent for "e.g." – it's best to include the examples as part of the text.

**Key Considerations for UVL:**

*   **Structure:** UVL is designed to represent structured information, so using sections and headings is crucial.
*   **Textual Content:**  UVL primarily focuses on the *content* of the guide.  It doesn't handle interactive elements like buttons or dropdowns.  This code provides a basic structure for displaying the information.
*   **Examples:**  The "e.g." examples are included directly within the text, as UVL doesn't have a way to represent them explicitly.
*   **No Formatting:** UVL doesn't support rich text formatting (bold, italics, lists, etc.).

This UVL code provides a solid foundation for presenting the PC configuration guide in a structured and readable way. You can then use this UVL data to render it within a UVL-compatible UI framework.